.SH NAME
ktm-net.https \- https:// for the HTTP client and TLS for servers.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
import net.https;
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
https:// for the HTTP client and TLS for servers.
.SH FUNCTIONS
.SS listen
.EX
i32 listen(i32 port, http.Mux *mux, u8[] certFile, u8[] keyFile, void *app = null, http.Options *opts = null);
.EE
.PP
As http.listen, over TLS with the certificate chain and private key in PEM files certFile and keyFile. Throws tls.ERR_TLS when they can't be read or don't belong together; otherwise as http.listen: serves until SIGINT, SIGTERM or http.shutdown(), then returns 0 (-1 with os.errno() when the port can't be used).
.SS listenConfig
.EX
i32 listenConfig(i32 port, http.Mux *mux, tls.Config *cfg, void *app = null, http.Options *opts = null);
.EE
.PP
The same with a tls.Config of your own (client certificates through caFile, a key password, ALPN...)
.SS setClientConfig
.EX
void setClientConfig(tls.Config *cfg);
.EE
.PP
The TLS settings of https:// requests from now on (a CA of your own, a client certificate); by default the system's CAs. Call it before the first request: connections already open keep theirs.
